turn functional.one_hot(pairs, 97).reshape(-1, 194).float()\ntask/evaluate.py:811:            controls = {\"constant\": state[\"constant\"], \"copy\": state[\"copy\"],\ntask/evaluate.py:818:                    \"parameter_count\": sum(parameter.numel() for parameter in model.parameters()), \"controls\": controls}\ntask/evaluate.py:828:            model = torch.nn.Sequential(torch.nn.Linear(194, width), torch.nn.ReLU(), torch.nn.Linear(width, 97))\ntask/evaluate.py:846:                     \"optimizer\": torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=.001, weight_decay=request[\"decay\"],\ntask/evaluate.py:852:            if state[\"step\"] + request[\"steps\"] > state[\"horizon\"]:\ntask/evaluate.py:856:            for _ in range(request[\"steps\"]):\ntask/evaluate.py:875:    horizon, block_steps = (16, 2) if smoke else (20000, 500)\ntask/evaluate.py:940:            recipe = call({\"op\": \"recipe\", \"steps\": horizon, \"block_steps\": block_steps, \"min_lr\": .0001, \"max_lr\": .01})\ntask/evaluate.py:941:            if type(recipe) is not dict or set(recipe) != {\"initial_lr\", \"decay_rate\"} or any(\ntask/evaluate.py:942:                not finite_number(value) for value in recipe.values()) or not .0001 <= recipe[\"initial_lr\"] <= .01 or not .01 <= recipe[\"decay_rate\"] <= 1:\ntask/evaluate.py:944:            normalized = (float(recipe[\"initial_lr\"]), float(recipe[\"decay_rate\"]))\ntask/evaluate.py:963:            for step in range(0, horizon, block_steps):\ntask/evaluate.py:968:                response = call({\"op\": \"schedule\", \"step\": step, \"steps\": horizon, \"block_steps\": block_steps})\ntask/evaluate.py:974:                trace.append(train_call({\"op\": \"train\", \"lr\": response[\"lr\"], \"steps\": block_steps}))\ntask/evaluate.py:975:            if trace[-1][\"step\"] != horizon or len(trace) != horizon // block_steps + 1:\n# Capacity versus decay clock: interface-limited candidate\n\nAttempt: `cycle_01_capacity_decay_clock_attempt_01`  \nIdea: `cycle_01_capacity_decay_clock`  \nProgram: `program_1`\n\n## Implemented control\n\n`program.py:handle` retains the starter's exact reset/recipe/schedule contract,\nvalidation and state transitions. Every seed and workload receives the same\nrecipe: `initial_lr=0.01`, `decay_rate=1.0`. The prescribed schedule therefore\nreturns the constant rate `0.01`. There are no extra response fields, external\ndependencies, hidden observations or self-owned training jobs.\n\nThe scientific motivation is a fixed-rate decay clock. For constant learning\nrate eta and fixed AdamW decay lambda with 0 <= eta*lambda < 1, the cumulative\nshrinkage clock is `-step*log1p(-eta*lambda)`. Raising eta increases that clock\nper update when lambda is positive; zero decay remains zero. This algebra does\nnot imply improved accuracy: changing eta also changes gradient updates and\nstability. The recipe uses the permitted maximum to provide more decay exposure\nwithin the fixed training budget, not a measured optimum. It differs from the\noriginal proposed learning rate of 0.001 and is not a confirmatory test of that\nproposal. The actual decay values are not supplied to this controller.\n\n## Scientific work not implementable through this interface\n\nThe proposed experiment uses modular addition p=31, training fraction 0.4,\nwidths 16/32/64/128 and five paired seeds. It requires label-free initial-logit\ncalibration, a separate pilot, independently randomized-label fitting controls,\nzero/positive-decay interventions, sustained 0.99 crossing measurements,\nadministrative censoring, paired-seed bootstrap intervals and saved run records.\nNone of these controls or observations is exposed by the RPC interface.\nThe documented evaluator instead owns its 194-input/97-logit network family,\ndata, optimizer, training and measurements. This candidate does not substitute\nthe proposed settings, invent additional RPC inputs, or claim to run those\nexperiments. It cannot log an exact observed decay clock without decay inputs.\n\nThe original falsifiable prediction remains untested: wider fitting-capable\nnetworks should fit true and random labels earlier, while true-label\ngeneralization times remain within a factor of two over an eightfold width\nrange, with an uncertainty interval contained inside that equivalence region.\nA fixed-rate controller alone cannot establish, reject or quantify that claim.\n\n## Evidence and reproduction status\n\nNo candidate code, tests, training, optimizer, benchmark or evaluation was\nexecuted during preparation. No performance improvement, confidence interval,\npaper, figure, or empirical research conclusion is claimed. The harness owns\nthe subsequent official evaluation. No external sources were retrieved and no\nforbidden literature was encountered. Reproduction of the implemented policy\nrequires only the starter's documented RPC sequence; seed values affect neither\nthe recipe nor the schedule. A research paper would require actual experiments\nand evidence not available at this stage.\nDesign candidate improvements within the verified open-loop learning-rate controller: use one seed-independent bounded exponential recipe across all evaluator-owned widths, operations, and decay arms, preserving the exact normalized schedule and RPC contract. Do not propose candidate-owned width sweeps, label controls, logit calibration, weight-decay changes, or feedback adaptation. Do not repeat strong annealing as an evidence-backed improvement: its recorded aggregate was below the constant-rate incumbent despite accepted correctness. Weaker annealing at the incumbent initial rate remains an untested controlled contrast, not a demonstrated improvement; the available scores establish neither monotonicity nor statistical significance. Learning-rate changes also alter cumulative AdamW shrinkage, so do not attribute aggregate gains solely to optimization. Use the canonical incumbent-improvement gate; neither an aggregate outcome nor a failed rubric gate identifies a width-dependent grokking mechanism or a failure cause. Reserve trajectory-based explanations, uncertainty claims, and paper-level scientific conclusions for explicitly available observations and actual experiments.\n2:\"\"\"Append validated local/global memory entries for idea workflows.\n6:* default (event append) — appends one memory event to ``memory.md`` (local +\n222:    parser.add_argument(\n228:    parser.add_argument(\"--event-json\", default=None, help=\"Event payload as JSON object string\")\n229:    parser.add_argument(\"--event-file\", default=None, help=\"Path to event JSON file\")\n230:    parser.add_argument(\n231:        \"--local-memory-md\",\n235:    parser.add_argument(\n236:        \"--global-memory-md\",\n240:    parser.add_argument(\n246:    parser.add_argument(\n251:    parser.add_argument(\n256:    parser.add_argument(\n261:    parser.add_argument(\n267:    parser.add_argument(\n273:    parser.add_argument(\n278:    parser.add_argument(\n390:            \"append_memory: --global-memory-md and --global-memory-jsonl are required \"\n400:    local_memory_path = Path(args.local_memory_md)\n404:    _append_text(local_memory_path, _markdown_entry(event), header=LOCAL_MEMORY_HEADERS)\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 10409 Nov 18 00:09 global_memory/memory.jsonl\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 12242 Nov 18 00:09 global_memory/memory.md\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
